# Messages of lib/checks/50-services.sh. Format and rules: see common.lang.
# A "unit" is anything systemd (the program that starts and supervises the
# system's services) manages: a service, a scheduled task, a mount...

section.50-services=Services

# ---- SVC-ALL ----
SVC-ALL.partial.1=systemd is not the running init system (container?), service checks skipped

# ---- SVC-FAILED ----
SVC-FAILED.warn.title={n} failed system unit(s)
SVC-FAILED.warn.impact=systemd is the program that starts and watches the system's services; each service or task it manages is called a unit. A failed unit did not start or stopped with an error: what it provides (printing, a network share, a backup...) may not work. It can also be a harmless leftover of software you no longer use.
SVC-FAILED.warn.act.1=Shows what the unit is, its state and its last messages. Read-only.
SVC-FAILED.warn.act.2=Shows all the messages of the unit since the computer started, to find the cause of the error. Read-only.
SVC-FAILED.warn.act.3=Once the cause is fixed, the unit leaves this list the next time it starts successfully (at the latest at the next restart of the computer).
SVC-FAILED.warn.caution=Do not disable or remove a unit you do not know before finding out what it does: search for its name first. Some units are needed to start the computer.
SVC-FAILED.ev.1=Failed units
SVC-FAILED.ok.title=No failed system unit
SVC-FAILED.partial.1=failed units not listed (systemctl failed)

# ---- SVC-USER-FAILED ----
SVC-USER-FAILED.warn.title={n} failed user unit(s)
SVC-USER-FAILED.warn.impact=These units are services or tasks that systemd starts for your session only (not for the whole system). A failed one did not start or stopped with an error: the program it belongs to may not work as expected.
SVC-USER-FAILED.warn.act.1=Shows what the unit is, its state and its last messages. Read-only.
SVC-USER-FAILED.warn.act.2=Shows all the messages of the unit since the computer started, to find the cause of the error. Read-only.
SVC-USER-FAILED.warn.act.3=Once the cause is fixed, the unit leaves this list the next time it starts successfully (at the latest at your next login).
SVC-USER-FAILED.ev.1=Failed user units

# ---- SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN ----
# A socket unit listens for connections on behalf of a service and starts it
# on the first connection (socket activation).
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.warn.title={n} active socket(s) whose service no longer exists
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.warn.impact=A socket unit listens for connections on behalf of a service and starts that service when the first connection arrives. Here the socket still listens, but its service no longer exists, usually because the package that provided it was removed. Every program that connects to it gets an error, and the socket stays open for nothing.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.warn.act.1=Shows the socket, its state, where it listens and its last messages. Read-only.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.warn.act.2=Shows the socket's settings file and its location, which often tells which software it belonged to. Read-only.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.warn.act.3=Closes the socket now and no longer opens it at boot. Can be undone: 'sudo systemctl enable --now' followed by the same name opens it again.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.warn.caution=If you have just removed or are reinstalling the software, a reinstall brings the service back and fixes this by itself. Check with 'dpkg -S' followed by the file shown by 'systemctl cat' whether a package still provides the socket.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.ev.1=Active sockets and the missing service
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.ok.title=Every active socket has its service
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.partial.1=active sockets not checked (systemctl failed)
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.user.title={n} active user socket(s) whose service no longer exists
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.user.impact=A socket unit listens for connections on behalf of a service and starts that service when the first connection arrives. This one belongs to your session: it still listens, but its service no longer exists, usually because the software that provided it was removed. Every program that connects to it gets an error.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.user.act.3=Closes the socket now and no longer opens it at login. Can be undone: 'systemctl --user enable --now' followed by the same name opens it again.
SVC-SOCKET-ORPHAN.ev.2=Active user sockets and the missing service

# ---- SVC-NETWORK ----
SVC-NETWORK.info.title={n} network-facing service(s) enabled at boot — confirm each is still needed
SVC-NETWORK.info.impact=Each of these services starts with the computer and can accept connections from other machines. A service started once for a test and then forgotten can stay open for years, and every open service is a way in if it has a flaw.
SVC-NETWORK.info.act.1=Shows what the service is, whether it is running and its last messages. Read-only.
SVC-NETWORK.info.act.2=Stops the service now and no longer starts it at boot, if you no longer use it. Can be undone: 'sudo systemctl enable --now' followed by the same name starts it again.
SVC-NETWORK.info.caution=Compare with the listening ports in the 'Network exposure' section (NET-LISTEN): they show which of these services really accept connections. If you connect to this computer remotely, do not disable the service you connect with (ssh, remote desktop): you would lose access.
SVC-NETWORK.ev.1=Enabled services (current state)
SVC-NETWORK.ok.title=No common network-facing service enabled at boot
